WEBVTT


00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:04.640
These beautiful stitch
patterns have so many uses.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.200
You often see them in the hand knitting
world, but as machine knitters, they're

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.580
completely do-able.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:17.150
I call these center double decreases, but
keep in mind, no stitches are actually

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:17.930
decreased.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.360
The number of stitches
in work remain the same.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.820
We're just doing some hand manipulation
to create texture and interest.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.470
Here's an example of a
center double decrease.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.629
The handling instructions
might call this a PSSO.

00:00:39.919 --> 00:00:45.919
The instructions might read, slip one,
knit two together, pass the slip, stitch.

00:00:45.919 --> 00:00:50.160
Over the hand knitting
chart would look like this.

00:00:51.759 --> 00:00:55.019
Notice that direction at
the front stitches laying.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:07.000
On the machine, to create this design, we
need to shuffle three stitches around so

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.180
that front stitch is in front.

00:01:12.215 --> 00:01:17.315
For this example, I've identified these
needles as the center of my double

00:01:17.315 --> 00:01:18.035
decreases.

00:01:20.975 --> 00:01:25.595
Start by moving the marked needle to
the left, doubling up two stitches.

00:01:26.865 --> 00:01:31.274
Then moved these two stitches
back to that marked center needle.

00:01:33.014 --> 00:01:37.214
Next, transfer the right
stitch over those two stitches.

00:01:37.845 --> 00:01:43.035
So you've got three stitches on the
center needle and two emptied needles.

00:01:44.774 --> 00:01:49.035
Now I'll repeat this across the
row, working on my marked needles.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:05.150
All this shuffling around, put a single
stitch leaning left on the front side of

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:05.720
the knitting.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.800
Notice that there's still
two emptied needles.

00:02:12.035 --> 00:02:17.825
To complete the design, I'll use a three
prong tool to transfer three stitches to

00:02:17.825 --> 00:02:20.165
fill in those newly emptied needles.

00:02:20.674 --> 00:02:24.614
Now you could move any number of stitches
I just chose three for, because I have a

00:02:24.614 --> 00:02:26.075
three-prong transfer tool.

00:02:27.334 --> 00:02:33.305
This creates eyelets away from the
decrease, and that's what creates our

00:02:33.305 --> 00:02:34.084
design.

00:02:38.915 --> 00:02:46.445
I've set up my stitch pattern to have
a single stitch bordered by eyelets on

00:02:46.535 --> 00:02:48.575
either side of that double decrease.

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:52.265
Get creative and do whatever you'd like.

00:02:52.565 --> 00:02:58.715
We just need to account for those eyelets
and position them in a decorative manner.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:03.630
After you work this stitch pattern for a
few rows, notice how the stitches seem to

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.250
move at a diagonal.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.370
This is what I think makes this a
really interesting stitch pattern.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.260
One more time.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.250
You've identified your center stitch,
you're going to transfer that center

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:24.300
stitch to the left, transfer the two
stitches back to the center, transfer the

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.730
single right stitch over to the center.

00:03:27.465 --> 00:03:31.065
Making sure you have three
stitches on that center needle.

00:03:33.595 --> 00:03:38.545
Fill in the empty needles,
knit two rows and repeat.

00:03:41.895 --> 00:03:46.215
Now that you've seen all those stitch
transfers, could you do this with a lace

00:03:46.215 --> 00:03:46.905
carriage?

00:03:47.235 --> 00:03:47.805
Of course!

00:03:48.735 --> 00:03:56.575
The challenge would be to punch a card or
program your machine for multiple stitch

00:03:56.605 --> 00:03:57.595
transfers.

00:03:57.804 --> 00:04:00.505
And make sure they all go
in the right direction.

00:04:02.245 --> 00:04:07.095
Now another option for this would be to
have the decrease leaning to the right.

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:09.385
In hand knitting,

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:11.995
this would be knit three together.

00:04:13.575 --> 00:04:16.865
For machine knitters, we would just
transfer the stitches in the opposite

00:04:16.865 --> 00:04:17.495
direction.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.680
Here's another popular option.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:31.070
Manipulate those stitches so
the center stitch is in front.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:36.860
When you're looking at the work from the
knit side, and that center stitch creates

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:37.760
a column.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.100
I'm using needle number five as my center.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:46.800
I'll transfer one stitch right.

00:04:47.335 --> 00:04:54.145
And one stitch left ending up with three
stitches on that center needle again, but

00:04:54.145 --> 00:04:59.455
this time they're in a different sequence
and that center stitch is going to be in

00:04:59.455 --> 00:05:02.145
front when you look at
it from the right side.

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:08.535
I'll then fill in the empty needles, just
like before having eyelets away from that

00:05:08.535 --> 00:05:09.555
center stitch.

00:05:11.115 --> 00:05:12.795
Here's my column of stitches.

00:05:13.289 --> 00:05:16.830
And the diagonal appearance on each side.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:22.530
This is just one little taste of
what you can do with this technique.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.349
I suggest you draw it out first.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:30.239
Use the little stitch symbols to indicate
which direction you're moving stitches.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:36.060
I challenge you to play with stitch
transfers and make your own design.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:37.950
Happy knitting!

